mptcp-bpf-first (1.4-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Read the subflow stale flag through CO-RE (BPF_CORE_READ_BITFIELD).
    Linux 6.18.55 turned mptcp_subflow_context.stale from a 1-bit field
    into a u8, and the plain subflow->stale was compiled for the bitfield
    of the shipped vmlinux.h: libbpf refused to relocate it and the
    scheduler did not load. The CO-RE read fits both layouts.

 -- Yannick Chabanois <contact@openmptcprouter.com>  Fri, 23 Oct 2026 09:49:06 +0000

mptcp-bpf-first (1.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Retransmit like the default scheduler: call mptcp_set_timeout() on every
    pick and add a get_retrans that prefers a subflow with nothing
    outstanding at TCP level, and a backup one only when no subflow makes
    progress. Without them the MPTCP retransmission timer stayed at
    TCP_RTO_MIN (200 ms) whatever the paths' round-trip times, and each time
    it fired get_send sent the oldest unacknowledged data again, usually on
    the path already holding it (measured on two shaped 8 Mbit/s paths:
    goodput 4.8 -> 8.0 Mbit/s).

 -- Yannick Chabanois <contact@openmptcprouter.com>  Fri, 09 Oct 2026 13:14:19 +0000

mptcp-bpf-first (1.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fall back to the first usable subflow when msk->first cannot send. The
    scheduler keeps its single-path character -- msk->first is still used
    whenever it can carry data -- but no longer insists on a subflow whose WAN
    is blackholed, which stopped transfers for as long as TCP took to give up
    (measured as a 24 s stall and 3.6x the payload on the wire).

 -- Yannick Chabanois <contact@openmptcprouter.com>  Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000

